---
import HomeFooter from '../components/home/HomeFooter.astro';
import HomeHeader from '../components/home/HomeHeader.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About ControlZebra"
	description="Learn about ControlZebra, version control designed for automation teams."
	themeColor="#12161c"
	bodyClass="homepage"
>
	<div class="home-shell about-page">
		<HomeHeader />
		<main class="about-content">
			<header class="about-intro">
				<span class="about-logo" aria-hidden="true">Z</span>
				<h1>About ControlZebra</h1>
				<p>
					Clear version history for the full lifecycle of the systems that keep factories moving.
				</p>
			</header>

			<div class="about-sections">
				<section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="mission-title">
					<h2 id="mission-title">Our Mission</h2>
					<p>
						ControlZebra exists to help industrial teams make every change interpretable throughout the
						full lifecycle of their projects. From design and commissioning to maintenance and future
						upgrades, teams should be able to see what changed, understand why it changed, and act with
						confidence.
					</p>
					<p>
						We build practical tools for change visualization, safe rollback, traceability, and approvals
						so that a project history supports better decisions instead of becoming another system to
						manage.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="who-we-are-title">
					<h2 id="who-we-are-title">Who We Are</h2>
					<p>
						ControlZebra was started by an industrial automation engineer turned product manager, with the
						support of colleagues and friends who know the pressure of keeping real systems running. It
						grew from a frustration shared by engineers everywhere: the tools needed to manage critical
						project history are often beyond the reach of smaller teams.
					</p>
					<p>
						We believe industrial software should work with open standards such as XML and give teams
						the freedom to use the Git host that fits their work. That is how more people can understand,
						protect, and improve the systems they are trusted to maintain.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
					<h2 id="roadmap-title">Roadmap</h2>
					<ol class="roadmap-stages" aria-label="ControlZebra product roadmap">
								<li class="roadmap-stage roadmap-stage-current">
									<p class="roadmap-phase">Today</p>
									<h3>Desktop workflow</h3>
									<p>Git workflow, visual L5X diffs, history, and rollback.</p>
								</li>
								<li class="roadmap-stage">
									<p class="roadmap-phase">Open Foundation</p>
									<h3>Parser library</h3>
									<p>An open-source parser library for industrial project files.</p>
								</li>
								<li class="roadmap-stage">
									<p class="roadmap-phase">Industrial support</p>
									<h3>Siemens TIA Portal</h3>
									<p>Project support for Siemens TIA Portal.</p>
								</li>
								<li class="roadmap-stage">
									<p class="roadmap-phase">Git ecosystem</p>
									<h3>GitLab and Gitea</h3>
									<p>Support for the Git hosts teams choose for their work.</p>
								</li>
								<li class="roadmap-stage">
									<p class="roadmap-phase">Beyond</p>
									<h3>More industrial systems</h3>
									<p>PLC, network switch, HMI, and SCADA project support.</p>
								</li>
					</ol>
				</section>
			</div>
		</main>
		<HomeFooter />
	</div>
</BaseLayout>